  {
    "parts": [
      {
        "content": "## Summary Report\n\n**Target Fact:** `F:ml430-nat-add-modeq-left-e3b1fba9`  \n**Formal Statement:** `∀ {n a : ℕ}, n + a ≡ a [MOD n]`\n\n**Structural Shape:** This is a modular equivalence statement asserting that for any natural numbers n and a, adding n to a is congruent to a modulo n. The goal has universal quantification over two natural number variables with no dependencies.\n\n**Dependencies:** The fact has zero dependencies (`depends_on: []`) and would unlock nothing when closed. It's in the development partition with \"backlog\" band status.\n\n**Available Premises:** The nat prelude contains 249 theorems total. Relevant available premises include:\n- Addition theorems: `Nat.add_comm`, `Nat.add_assoc`, `Nat.add_zero`, `Nat.add_succ`, cancellation laws\n- One modEq theorem: `Nat.modEq_pow` (congruence preservation under exponentiation)\n- Notably, the kernel has very limited modEq infrastructure—no definition or basic properties are visible\n\n**Similar Eligible Facts:**\n1. `F:ml430-nat-add-modeq-right-e2f11f21`: `∀ {n a : ℕ}, a + n ≡ a [MOD n]` (commuted version)\n2. `F:ml430-int-add-modeq-left-ee732b5b`: `∀ {n a : ℤ}, n + a ≡ a [ZMOD n]` (Int version)\n3. `F:ml430-int-add-modeq-right-e58108ee`: `∀ {n a : ℤ}, a + n ≡ a [ZMOD n]` (Int commuted)\n\n**Registry Status:** No operations currently target this fact.",
        "id": null,
        "provider_name": null,
        "provider_details": null,
        "part_kind": "text"
      }
